{"content_hash": "638e32701eae0a0b", "version_id": 201, "article_id": 21, "level_code": "en_a1", "lang": "en", "level": 1, "level_label": "A1 入门", "title": "Spanish woman, 87, dies after eviction", "title_zh": "西班牙87岁老奶奶被赶出家门后去世", "topic": "world", "source": "bbc_world", "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6e3x21ev9wwo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/standard/240/cpsprodpb/d9ee/live/06010330-c283-11f1-98ea-35e6bf307fc9.jpg", "credit": ""}, "published_date": "2026-10-08", "word_count": 240, "reading_minutes": 1.2, "lead": "An old woman in Spain lost her home. She could not pay the new rent. Now she has died, and many people are angry.", "lead_zh": "西班牙一位老奶奶失去了自己的家，因为她付不起新的房租。现在她去世了，很多人非常愤怒。", "preview": "Maricarmen Abascal was 87 years old. She lived in her home in Madrid for more than 70 years. On 23 September, police took her out of her hom", "preview_zh": "玛丽卡门·阿瓦斯卡尔87岁。她住在马德里自己的家里70多年。9月23日，警察把她从家里带走。她付不起新的房租。房租从每月500欧元涨到了2650欧元。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 191, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@e099b16/content/audio/21/en_a1.mp3", "duration": 98.837, "size_bytes": 596736, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 286, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@e099b16/content/data/versions/201.json", "paragraphs": ["Maricarmen Abascal was 87 years old. She lived in her home in Madrid for more than 70 years. On 23 September, police took her out of her home. She could not pay the new rent. The rent went up from 500 euros to 2,650 euros each month.", "Her family rented the flat in 1956. After her parents died, she stayed there. A company bought the building in 2018. The company later said she could come back and pay less. But she was in hospital and could not sign the paper.", "On 7 October, she died in hospital. The Madrid Tenants' Union told everyone the sad news. A union leader said: \"Her memory lives with us.\" People in Madrid were very sad. They went to Puerta del Sol, a big square in the city.", "Thousands of people have camped in that square for 12 nights. They want cheaper homes. On Wednesday night, they lit candles for Maricarmen. They held signs. One sign said: \"It is not a death, it is a killing.\" Then they marched to the Spanish Congress.", "The housing problem is now a big political problem in Spain. Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez called an early election for 29 November. Unions also called a general strike for 11 November. They want new housing laws.", "In a video from her hospital bed, Maricarmen said: \"I did not save my home. But I fought so other people can fight for their homes.\""], "paragraphs_zh": ["玛丽卡门·阿瓦斯卡尔87岁。她住在马德里自己的家里70多年。9月23日，警察把她从家里带走。她付不起新的房租。房租从每月500欧元涨到了2650欧元。", "她的家人1956年租下了这套房子。父母去世后，她继续住在这里。2018年，一家公司买下了这栋楼。公司后来说，她可以回来住，房租也可以少一些。但她当时在医院，没法签文件。", "10月7日，她在医院去世了。马德里租户联盟把这个难过的消息告诉大家。一位工会负责人说：“她的记忆和我们在一起。”马德里的人们非常伤心。他们去了市中心的太阳门广场。", "几千人已经在这个广场上住了12个晚上。他们想要更便宜的房子。星期三晚上，他们为玛丽卡门点了蜡烛。他们举着牌子。一块牌子上写着：“这不是死亡，这是杀害。”然后他们向西班牙国会走去。", "住房问题现在是西班牙很大的政治问题。首相佩德罗·桑切斯宣布11月29日提前大选。工会也号召11月11日举行大罢工。他们想要新的住房法律。", "在一段从医院病床拍的视频里，玛丽卡门说：“我没能保住我的家。但我努力了，让别人可以为自己的家去争取。”"], "body": "Maricarmen Abascal was 87 years old. She lived in her home in Madrid for more than 70 years. On 23 September, police took her out of her home. She could not pay the new rent. The rent went up from 500 euros to 2,650 euros each month.\n\nHer family rented the flat in 1956. After her parents died, she stayed there. A company bought the building in 2018. The company later said she could come back and pay less. But she was in hospital and could not sign the paper.\n\nOn 7 October, she died in hospital. The Madrid Tenants' Union told everyone the sad news. A union leader said: \"Her memory lives with us.\" People in Madrid were very sad. They went to Puerta del Sol, a big square in the city.\n\nThousands of people have camped in that square for 12 nights. They want cheaper homes. On Wednesday night, they lit candles for Maricarmen. They held signs. One sign said: \"It is not a death, it is a killing.\" Then they marched to the Spanish Congress.\n\nThe housing problem is now a big political problem in Spain. Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez called an early election for 29 November. Unions also called a general strike for 11 November. They want new housing laws.\n\nIn a video from her hospital bed, Maricarmen said: \"I did not save my home. But I fought so other people can fight for their homes.\"", "body_zh": "玛丽卡门·阿瓦斯卡尔87岁。她住在马德里自己的家里70多年。9月23日，警察把她从家里带走。她付不起新的房租。房租从每月500欧元涨到了2650欧元。\n\n她的家人1956年租下了这套房子。父母去世后，她继续住在这里。2018年，一家公司买下了这栋楼。公司后来说，她可以回来住，房租也可以少一些。但她当时在医院，没法签文件。\n\n10月7日，她在医院去世了。马德里租户联盟把这个难过的消息告诉大家。一位工会负责人说：“她的记忆和我们在一起。”马德里的人们非常伤心。他们去了市中心的太阳门广场。\n\n几千人已经在这个广场上住了12个晚上。他们想要更便宜的房子。星期三晚上，他们为玛丽卡门点了蜡烛。他们举着牌子。一块牌子上写着：“这不是死亡，这是杀害。”然后他们向西班牙国会走去。\n\n住房问题现在是西班牙很大的政治问题。首相佩德罗·桑切斯宣布11月29日提前大选。工会也号召11月11日举行大罢工。他们想要新的住房法律。\n\n在一段从医院病床拍的视频里，玛丽卡门说：“我没能保住我的家。但我努力了，让别人可以为自己的家去争取。”", "text": "Maricarmen Abascal was 87 years old. She lived in her home in Madrid for more than 70 years. On 23 September, police took her out of her home. She could not pay the new rent. The rent went up from 500 euros to 2,650 euros each month. Her family rented the flat in 1956. After her parents died, she stayed there. A company bought the building in 2018. The company later said she could come back and pay less. But she was in hospital and could not sign the paper. On 7 October, she died in hospital. The Madrid Tenants' Union told everyone the sad news. A union leader said: \"Her memory lives with us.\" People in Madrid were very sad. They went to Puerta del Sol, a big square in the city. Thousands of people have camped in that square for 12 nights. They want cheaper homes. On Wednesday night, they lit candles for Maricarmen. They held signs. One sign said: \"It is not a death, it is a killing.\" Then they marched to the Spanish Congress. The housing problem is now a big political problem in Spain. Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez called an early election for 29 November. Unions also called a general strike for 11 November. They want new housing laws. In a video from her hospital bed, Maricarmen said: \"I did not save my home. But I fought so other people can fight for their homes.\"", "vocab": [{"word": "rent", "pos": "noun", "zh": "房租", "note": "每月付给房东的钱"}, {"word": "home", "pos": "noun", "zh": "家；住宅", "note": "这里指住的地方"}, {"word": "die", "pos": "verb", "zh": "去世；死亡", "note": "过去式是 died"}, {"word": "police", "pos": "noun", "zh": "警察", "note": "常用复数形式"}, {"word": "hospital", "pos": "noun", "zh": "医院", "note": "in hospital 表示住院"}, {"word": "candle", "pos": "noun", "zh": "蜡烛", "note": "常用来表示哀悼"}, {"word": "election", "pos": "noun", "zh": "选举", "note": "early election 指提前大选"}, {"word": "fight", "pos": "verb", "zh": "斗争；争取", "note": "过去式是 fought"}], "title_original": "Spanish pensioner whose eviction sparked nationwide protests dies, union says", "summary_original": "Maricarmen Abascal, 87, was forcibly removed on a stretcher from her apartment of more than 70 years in September.", "body_original": "Spanish pensioner whose eviction sparked nationwide protests dies, union says\nBy\nAlys Davies\nand\nAlba Morgade in Madrid\nPublished\n7 October 2026\nUpdated 59 minutes ago\nAn elderly woman whose eviction sparked nationwide protests over Spain's housing crisis and a political row that triggered early elections has died, according to Madrid's tenants union.\nMaricarmen Abascal, 87, was forcibly removed on a stretcher from her apartment of more than 70 years on 23 September after she was unable to afford rent increases.\nThe company that owned the flat later offered to allow her to return at a lower rate - but she was unable to sign an agreement as she had been admitted to hospital.\n\"We regret to announce that Maricarmen passed away today,\" the Madrid Tenants' Union said in a statement, adding her situation marked \"a turning point in the history of this country\".\nUnion spokesperson Alicia del Río said: \"Speculation costs lives, and today rentierism has finished killing our colleague Maricarmen\" but \"her memory lives on with us\".\nA cause of death was not announced.\nAbascal's death was received in Puerta del Sol with a big collective gasp, then followed by a minute of silence.\nIt is in this square in the centre of Madrid where thousands have been camping since Abascal's eviction last month to demand more affordable housing.\nOn Wednesday, it became the place to pay tribute to her.\nPeople lit dozens of candles around the square in a small improvised vigil that glowed with a warm light on a dry and warm autumn night.\nOther candles formed an altar to Abascal in front of the headquarters of the Community of Madrid government.\nMessages on cardboard banners next to the candles read \"it's not a death, it's a killing\", \"the fight will continue\" and \"Mari Carmen, the grandmother of the whole country\", among others.\nA few hundred people then took to the streets that connect Puerta del Sol with the Spanish Congress, just 500m away, in an improvised protest.\nDuring the march people chanted: \"We will not tolerate a single eviction more\" and \"the fight will continue, Mari Carmen is still alive\". There were other chants against fascism and against the police.\nThe police had set up barriers to block the march. There was not a big police presence, and there was the tension that protests sometimes have.\nMany protesters seemed to feel the police have been complicit in the evictions, as under court orders they have to remove tenants who have been served eviction notices.\nAfter a couple of hours, the chants near Congress gave way to another minute of silence. Then, a big collective shout of \"murderers\".\nWednesday night's march has finished, but the camping, already on its 12th night, will continue \"as long as necessary\" one of the protesters told the BBC.\nAbascal became a symbol for Spain's housing crisis following her eviction.\nHer father first rented the property in 1956 and she continued as the tenant after her parents died, during which time there was a cap on the rent.\nHowever, Urbagestión, which bought the property in 2018, increased the rent from €500 per month (£428; $572) to €2,650 (£2,280; $3,020).\nThe company later offered to reduce this to €1,650 - still €300 more than Abascal's monthly pension.\nThree previous attempts to evict her were blocked by the courts and the case has received increasing media attention.\nAnger over the situation grew, leading to protests and putting pressure on the coalition government to approve emergency housing legislation -\nbut it was rejected in Congress last week\n.\nThat led to further protests,\nwith tens of thousands of people taking\nto the streets\nin cities across the country over the weekend.\nReferring to the rejected bills, the Madrid Tenants' Union said: \"While a historic agreement was reached that would have allowed her to return to her home, this solution has come too late. An absolutely avoidable situation after a torturing process.\"\nIt said the events surrounding her eviction represented a turning point in the country's history, but added that the cost to get to this point had been \"too high\".\n\"Above all, we will fulfil what she always asked of us: 'fight, keep fighting for what is yours',\" it said.\nIn a video recorded from her hospital bed following her eviction, Maricarmen said: \"I haven't managed to defend my home, but I have fought so that others can learn to fight for what's theirs.\"\n\"All my love goes to Maricarmen's family and loved ones,\" Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez said. \"A hug as well to everyone who today feels this loss as their own.\"\nOpposition People's Party leader Alberto Núñez Feijóo also sent his condolences to Maricarmen's loved ones, as he said on X: \"No campaign, no parties, no self-interested exploitation. No more stones in the wall.\n\"In the face of a person's death, only respect is in order,\" he added.\nEarlier on Wednesday, unions had called a general strike to take place on 11 November, demanding housing reforms.\nThe public outcry over Abascal's situation has led to political turmoil in Spain.\nOn Monday Sánchez, who leads a leftist coalition that does not hold a majority in parliament, called an early election for 29 November. It followed his failure to get the emergency housing legislation through Congress.\nThe political right had argued that the government's attempt to intervene in the housing market would have led to fewer homes being rented and would have raised prices further.\nSpeaking on Monday, Sánchez said he needed \"a broader progressive majority\" that can \"overcome the resistance of the elites\" to push through reforms.\nOther issues not relating to housing, such as numerous corruption scandals linked to his party, and pressures relating to the cost of living and immigration, have also caused him problems.\nMost opinion polls suggest the conservatives are likely to emerge as the largest party in the next election. However, they would be expected to need the support of the hard-right Vox party to form a government.\nAdditional reporting by Tom McArthur\nRelated topics\nSpain\nMore on this story\nSpanish PM Sánchez calls early election after housing protests\nPublished\n2 days ago\nDramatic eviction of woman aged 87 highlights Spain's housing shortage\nPublished\n23 September", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T01:16:22.071120+00:00", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "Maricarmen", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.85, "sm": 100, "em": 850, "cs": 0, "ce": 10, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "Abascal", "s": 0.863, "e": 1.363, "sm": 862, "em": 1362, "cs": 11, "ce": 18, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "was", "s": 1.375, "e": 1.613, "sm": 1375, "em": 1612, "cs": 19, "ce": 22, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "87", "s": 1.625, "e": 1.875, "sm": 1625, "em": 1875, "cs": 23, "ce": 25, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "years", "s": 1.875, "e":